package standard.io

import standard.wasi.ComponentStreamRead
import standard.wasi.OpenFlag
import standard.wasi.noteCopyProgress
import standard.wasi.CopyResult
import standard.wasi.IoError
import standard.text.Charset

/// The buffer one p3 copy asks for: 64 KiB.
///
/// Package-internal. It is the size this library allocates for one copy, not a
/// number a caller should have to know: `read` takes whatever length the caller
/// has a reason for and answers with at most that much. Callers elsewhere that
/// want "a chunk" name their own buffer size, and asking for more or less than
/// this costs nothing but a shorter answer.
///
/// A read's capacity is an ALLOCATION rather than a ceiling on the answer: the
/// runtime pinned-allocs exactly this many bytes of linear memory before posting
/// the copy (`emit_p3_stream_read_start`). So `read(Int64.max)` would ask for
/// 2 GiB of linear memory; large requests are served one chunk at a time
/// instead, and a caller that wants the whole stream loops — which is `readAll`.
let readChunkBytes: Int64 = 65536i64

/// Async, Resource-aware input stream over a byte transport. `WasiInputStream`
/// drives a p3 `stream<u8>`; other subclasses (a TLS stream, say) decrypt bytes
/// pulled from an underlying stream. The fs, socket, stdin and TLS libraries
/// hand this out.
///
/// THE CONTRACT. `AsyncOutputStream` states the same rules in the same order,
/// with the differences called out where they are real.
///
/// VOCABULARY. A stream is DEAD when it has recorded a transport failure,
/// FINISHED when it ended without one, and LIVE otherwise. An OPERATION is any
/// public method here; a COPY is one host transfer.
///
/// (1) EMPTY MEANS END OF STREAM, and nothing else means it. Every caller stops
///     on empty, so a copy that transferred nothing without ending the stream is
///     retried rather than reported — boundedly, see `readOpen`.
///
/// (2) NO OPERATION ANSWERS A DEAD STREAM WITH ANYTHING A CALLER READS AS CLEAN
///     SUCCESS, and an empty array — which (1) defines as the end of the stream
///     — is exactly that. A dead stream answers with the failure it recorded.
///
///     WHEN the question is asked differs by operation. One with nothing to
///     fetch (a zero-length read) has only the health question, so it asks it and
///     answers from it. One that can still reach the transport goes there FIRST
///     and reports the death only once nothing is left to hand over — that is
///     (3), and where (2) and (3) meet, (3) wins. What (2) forbids absolutely is
///     the other order: a dead stream answering empty.
///
///     The question is `checkAlive`, abstract so that no implementation can omit
///     it by saying nothing. A FINISHED stream is alive for this purpose and
///     answers empty, because empty is what an ending is. A stream somebody
///     CLOSED under a caller is not — see (9).
///
/// (3) A PAYLOAD TRANSFERRED IS A PAYLOAD DELIVERED. Whatever a copy moved is
///     handed to the caller whatever the copy's status, and the reason it
///     stopped is latched and reported by the NEXT operation. A `Dropped` copy
///     carrying the last chunk of a file is the case this exists for.
///
///     It binds the RUNTIME as much as this class: by the time an ending is
///     known the elements are already out of the pinned buffer, so a layer that
///     reports the failure in place of them is not deferring those bytes, it is
///     destroying them. `Async.streamRead` yields the ending alongside the
///     elements for that reason.
///
/// (4) CANCELLED IS THIS OPERATION, not the transport. A cancel that carried
///     nothing fails with the stream's own `canceledError`, leaves the stream
///     usable, and is never latched as an ending.
///
///     A cancel that DID carry something is not this operation failing: it is a
///     copy that completed too late to be stopped, and it arrives with its
///     elements and, if it also ended the stream, with the ending beside them.
///     (3) applies to it unchanged, and the fiber takes its interrupt after
///     handing the payload on. `Runtime.settleCancelledRead` states the rule for
///     both directions.
///
/// (5) A NEGATIVE LENGTH IS A CALLER BUG and panics, at the front door, before
///     the health check: underflowed arithmetic is a bug whether or not the
///     transport is healthy, and on a dead stream it would come back as a
///     plausible transport error and be blamed on the peer. The panic is a
///     deliberate trade — there is no way to synthesize a value of the abstract
///     error type `E` here — so a length computed from peer-controlled data
///     (`contentLength - alreadyRead` against a peer that oversends) is a remote
///     kill switch, and no caller in this tree may pass one through unclamped.
///
/// (6) EVERY OPERATION STOPS ON SOMETHING THE STREAM DECIDES: `read` at one
///     transport copy, `readAll` at the end of the stream. Neither stopping
///     condition is the caller's to supply, which is what makes them mean the
///     same thing on a file, a socket and a TLS session.
///
///     A read that stops on a byte COUNT is deliberately not offered. "How many
///     bytes do I need before I can make progress" is a fact about the caller's
///     parser rather than about the stream — a length-prefixed frame needs its
///     four bytes, an HTTP state machine needs whatever came, and both run over
///     the same socket. A stream that promised to fill a buffer could not serve
///     the second: on a socket it would block for bytes the peer will not send
///     until it has heard from us, which is a deadlock and not a slow read. So
///     the loop belongs to whoever named the count; `read` shows its shape.
///
/// (7) THE EXTENSION POINTS ARE `protected` AND THE PUBLIC API IS `final`.
///     `read` holds (5) and (8) for every transport, which is worth nothing if a
///     caller can reach past it to the unguarded hook.
///
/// (8) WHAT A SUBCLASS OWES THE BASE is (1), and never handing back more bytes
///     than it was asked for. It is given a validated, strictly positive length
///     and owes nothing else.
///
///     The second half is CHECKED rather than merely documented: `read` is
///     final, so it is the one place that can, and a subclass that overshoots
///     panics there. There is nowhere for the surplus to go — this class holds
///     no pushback buffer — and both silent alternatives are worse: dropping it
///     inverts (3), handing it over overruns whatever the caller sized from the
///     number it passed in.
///
/// (9) A STREAM CLOSED UNDER A CALLER IS NOT A STREAM THAT ENDED. `close`
///     detaches every fiber on the handle and fails each with the stream's
///     `canceledError`; a fiber that arrives an instant later is told the same
///     thing rather than handed the empty array that (1) defines as the peer
///     being done. Otherwise "did the peer finish, or did a sibling take the
///     stream out from under me" is answered by which side of the close the
///     scheduler put you on, and the losing side reports a truncated body as a
///     complete one.
public abstract class AsyncInputStream<E>
    where E: IoError =
    /// Read up to `bytesLength` bytes: ONE transport copy.
    ///
    /// FEWER bytes than asked for is ordinary and is not a signal — a socket
    /// answers with what the peer has sent so far, a file with what fits in one
    /// copy. A caller that needs a particular NUMBER of bytes loops until it has
    /// them; one that wants the whole stream uses `readAll`.
    ///
    ///     let got = ByteBuffer.make()
    ///     while got.length < wanted do
    ///         let chunk = await stream.read((wanted - got.length).toInt64())
    ///         if chunk.length == 0 then <the stream ended first> else got.append(chunk)
    ///
    /// The `chunk.length == 0` arm is the one to get right: empty means the
    /// stream ended (1), and a loop that stops on anything else — a short read,
    /// say — truncates.
    ///
    /// ZERO does no transport work, so it is not a readiness probe. It is still
    /// an operation and answers per (2): the recorded failure on a dead stream,
    /// an empty array otherwise. NEGATIVE panics, per (5).
    public final async function read(self, bytesLength: Int64): Async<Array<Uint8>, E> =
        if bytesLength < 0i64 then
            panic "input stream: read length must not be negative, got " ++ bytesLength.format()
        else
            if bytesLength == 0i64 then
                // The health check is the whole of a zero-length read: nothing is
                // asked for, so there is nothing that could be preferred to the
                // failure. The positive case below goes to the transport WITHOUT it,
                // because a dead transport may still be holding bytes that are the
                // caller's by (3).
                await self.checkAlive()
                Array<Uint8>.empty()
            else
                let chunk = await self.readAvailable(bytesLength)
                // (8): a subclass that hands back more than it was asked for has
                // taken those bytes off its transport and there is nothing this
                // class can do with them, so it is reported as the bug it is — named
                // and at the moment it happens, rather than as a wrong length
                // somewhere above.
                if chunk.length.toInt64() > bytesLength then
                    panic "input stream: a subclass returned "
                        ++ chunk.length.format()
                        ++ " bytes for a read of "
                        ++ bytesLength.format()
                else
                    chunk

    /// One transport read. `bytesLength` is strictly positive — the base
    /// validates it and handles both edges — and keeping (1) is this method's
    /// job, since only the transport knows what its end of stream is.
    ///
    /// Also this method's job and not the base's: a stream that is dead but
    /// still holding delivered bytes hands those over first and reports the
    /// death on the read after, per (3).
    protected abstract function readAvailable(self, bytesLength: Int64): Async<Array<Uint8>, E>

    /// Fail with the transport failure this stream recorded, if it recorded one.
    ///
    /// The one question behind (2), asked by every operation that has no bytes to
    /// go and get. A stream that has cleanly FINISHED is alive for this purpose —
    /// its end of stream is a legitimate answer — so this succeeds there and the
    /// empty array says the rest.
    ///
    /// Abstract rather than defaulted: the only default available ("nothing ever
    /// goes wrong here") is false for every transport that can fail, so a stream
    /// that really cannot die says so in one line and says it deliberately.
    protected abstract function checkAlive(self): Async<Unit, E>

    /// End this stream now and report what the transport made of it.
    ///
    /// Optional. The `Resource` that handed this stream out closes it on release
    /// whether or not this was called; what calling it adds is *when* (now, not
    /// at the end of the scope) and *what came of it* — a finalizer cannot fail,
    /// so it has nowhere to put a transport error. Using the stream afterwards
    /// is undefined.
    ///
    /// WHAT EVERY IMPLEMENTATION OWES, because it is the whole of (9) and a
    /// subclass that skips it turns a close into an end of stream: after this
    /// returns, no read on this stream answers with the empty array. It reports
    /// the stream's `canceledError` — or whatever this transport calls "somebody
    /// took this away from you" — for as long as anybody asks.
    ///
    /// WHAT IS THE TRANSPORT'S OWN BUSINESS is the read already in flight. A
    /// transport that owns its handle CANCELS it, in this fiber or another, rather
    /// than waiting: a read on a peer that never sends would never complete, so
    /// there is no in-flight operation that closing could promise to outlive, and
    /// those fibers fail with the origin's `Canceled`. A transport that does not
    /// own the handle it reads through — a TLS session over somebody else's socket
    /// — cannot, and lets the read in flight finish and be refused on its way out.
    /// The difference is visible to a caller in latency, never in the answer.
    public abstract function close(self): Async<Unit, E>

    /// Read until EOF, accumulating into a single byte array. Reads in
    /// 64 KiB chunks.
    public final async function readAll(self): Async<Array<Uint8>, E> =
        // A buffer rather than `accumulated.concat(chunk)`, which would re-copy
        // everything read so far on every chunk: O(n²) in the length.
        let accumulated = ByteBuffer.make()
        let mutable done = false
        while !done do
            let chunk = await self.read(readChunkBytes)
            if chunk.length == 0 then done = true else accumulated.append(chunk)
        accumulated.toArray()

    /// Read until EOF and decode the full content as the given charset.
    /// v1 only supports UTF-8.
    public final async function readStringAll(self, charset: Charset): Async<String, E> =
        match charset with
            case Charset.Utf8 =>
                let bytes = await self.readAll()
                String.fromBytes(bytes, 0, bytes.length)
            case _ => panic "unsupported charset"

/// Where a `Wasi*Stream` is in its life.
///
/// One value rather than separate flags for "no new ops", "the ending has been
/// read" and "the handles are gone", because those only ever move forwards and
/// only in this order. Spelling the order out is what makes the awkward rules
/// legible: why a failure means something different in `Closing` than in `Open`,
/// and why the ending is read on one path into `Released` and not the other.
///
/// Shared by both directions; `WasiOutputStream` reads more out of it, because
/// it has a result future to retire and the input side does not.
enum StreamState =
    /// Operations may post. The result future, if there is one, is unread.
    Open
    /// The stream ended on its own — a copy reported the end, or one failed — so
    /// the runtime has already consumed the result future. Nothing may post; the
    /// host handles are still ours to release.
    Ended
    /// `close` began on a stream that had not ended. Nothing may post, and the
    /// result future is still unread — `close` is about to read it.
    ///
    /// Distinct from `Ended` for one load-bearing reason: a failure arriving in
    /// this state is the close's own cancellation coming back, so it must NOT be
    /// recorded as how the stream ended. Recording it would make `close` report
    /// the error it caused in place of the one it exists to find.
    Closing
    /// A write stopped with part of its array on the transport and no way to
    /// finish it — cancelled, or interrupted where it was parked. Nothing may
    /// post: the byte stream has a hole in it, and a later write would splice
    /// itself onto a truncated prefix without anybody being told. But the stream
    /// did NOT end, so its result future is still unread and still owed a read.
    ///
    /// Which is why it is not `Ended`. The runtime consults the one-shot result
    /// future only for a copy that reports the stream `Dropped`, so `Ended` means
    /// "the runtime already spent it" and makes `dropHandles` skip the read —
    /// and a cancel spends nothing. Write-side only: a read cancelled mid-chunk
    /// leaves nothing behind for the next read to be confused by.
    Torn
    /// The host handles have been released. Terminal.
    Released

/// A p3-stream-backed input stream.
///
/// `source` describes the whole host operation — which readable end, how to post
/// a copy, how to lift the bytes, the result future that says why the stream
/// ended, and how to let all of it go. The runtime drives it; what is left here
/// is what the stream's type cannot say: the end-of-stream latch, and what a
/// caller gets for reading past it.
///
/// INTERNAL. It owns host handles, so it is only handed out attached to the
/// `Resource` that releases them — see `resource`. Callers outside this package
/// work with the abstract `AsyncInputStream` and never name this class.
internal class WasiInputStream<E>(
    public source: ComponentStreamRead<Array<Uint8>, E>,
    // The bit behind `source.stillOpen` — the record above was rebuilt with a
    // probe over this flag (`withStillOpen`, in `resource`). Flipped exactly
    // where `state` leaves the postable world: entering `Closing` and entering
    // `Released`, the two transitions a fiber OTHER than the lock-holding
    // reader can make. Not on `Ended`, which only the lock holder observes and
    // latches — the handle is still owned there, and the loop below never posts
    // past it. See `OpenFlag` for why this is a holder and not the state itself.
    openFlag: OpenFlag,
    // Reads post only in `Open`. Leaving it is what stops a second read
    // re-posting on a finished stream or re-reading its spent one-shot future,
    // either of which traps — and both are reachable from ordinary code:
    // `readAll()` then `read()`, a second `Console.readLine()` past EOF, an HTTP
    // keep-alive re-read.
    mutable state: StreamState,
    mutable finishError: Option<E>,
    // Held across a whole read. It is the state check that needs it, not the
    // ABI — see `readAvailable`.
    readLock: Mutex
) extends AsyncInputStream<E>()
    where E: IoError =
    // Every state but `Open` answers from the latch — on the way in, and again
    // before every posted copy (see the loop), because a sibling fiber can close
    // the stream between two copies. The alternative is not a wrong answer but a
    // wrong stream, since the host reuses a dropped handle's index.
    //
    // THE LOCK IS WHAT MAKES THAT CHECK WORTH ANYTHING when two fibers share the
    // stream. p3 allows one outstanding read per end and the runtime enforces
    // that with a per-handle lock taken INSIDE `Async.streamRead` — but a fiber
    // that finds it held is suspended on that node, and the grant re-runs the
    // node and posts. Nothing re-asks this class's question in between, so the
    // runtime's queue is a suspension point the state check cannot see across:
    // reader B, queued behind A while A's copy ends the stream, would be granted
    // the handle the instant A settles and post a copy on a stream whose
    // one-shot future has been spent, which traps. Serializing here puts the
    // check and the post in one critical section, and leaves the runtime's lock
    // uncontended as the ABI backstop it is meant to be.
    //
    // `checkAlive` deliberately does not take it: a zero-length read posts
    // nothing, so it has nothing to be ordered against, and making it wait behind
    // a read parked on a peer that never sends would turn the one operation that
    // answers immediately into one that never does.
    protected override async function readAvailable(self, bytesLength: Int64):
        Async<Array<Uint8>, E> =
        // `orPanicWith`: the only way to fail this acquire is for this fiber to
        // hold the lock already, and nothing here reads a stream from inside a
        // read of it. That is an invariant of this class rather than a condition
        // a caller could act on, so it stays out of `read`'s signature.
        let _held = use self
            .readLock
            .acquire()
            .orPanicWith((_: MutexError) => "WasiInputStream.read re-entered its own read lock")
        match self.state with
            case StreamState.Open => await self.readOpen(bytesLength)
            case _ => await Async.fromResult(self.latchedResult())

    /// How this stream died, if it did. A stream that ended cleanly is alive for
    /// this purpose — its EOF is an answer, not a failure — so only a latched
    /// transport error, or a close somebody else performed, is reported here.
    ///
    /// No copy is posted, and not only as an optimization: a zero-capacity copy
    /// comes back with a count of zero, which is the one answer `readOpen`
    /// refuses to believe, and on a stream whose ending has been read it would
    /// re-read a spent one-shot future and trap.
    protected override function checkAlive(self): Async<Unit, E> =
        match self.unusable() with
            case Some(error) => Async.fail(error)
            case None => Async.succeed(())

    /// Why this stream can no longer be read, if it cannot. The single statement
    /// of that rule; `checkAlive` and `latchedResult` are two spellings of it.
    ///
    /// Three endings, and they are not one answer. A recorded TRANSPORT FAILURE
    /// is reported as itself. A stream that ENDED with no failure is not unusable
    /// at all — its EOF is a legitimate answer, and (1) is how it is given. A
    /// stream somebody CLOSED reports `canceledError`, per (9): it did not end,
    /// it was taken away, and that is the error the runtime raised in whichever
    /// fibers were on the handle when `close` cancelled them.
    function unusable(self): Option<E> =
        match self.finishError with
            case Some(error) => Some(error)
            case None =>
                match self.state with
                    case StreamState.Closing => Some(self.source.canceledError)
                    case StreamState.Released => Some(self.source.canceledError)
                    case _ => None

    /// The read itself, for a stream that was `Open` on the way in.
    async function readOpen(self, bytesLength: Int64): Async<Array<Uint8>, E> =
        // One chunk per copy. See `readChunkBytes`: this number is a linear
        // memory allocation, not a limit on the answer.
        let capacity =
            if bytesLength > readChunkBytes then readChunkBytes.toInt32() else bytesLength.toInt32()
        let mutable delivered = Array<Uint8>.empty()
        let mutable emptyCopies = 0
        let mutable posting = true
        while posting do
            // Re-checked every time round rather than once on the way in: the
            // `await` below hands the scheduler back, so a sibling can run
            // `close()` between a copy settling and this loop re-posting. `close`
            // cancels and then DROPS the handle — and a read posted on a dropped
            // handle does not fail, it lands on whatever stream the host gave
            // that index to next. Falling out to the latch turns "too late" into
            // an answer instead of somebody else's bytes.
            //
            // THIS CHECK ALONE IS NOT ENOUGH, because it runs one interpreter
            // step before the copy is posted, and the fairness budget can run
            // the sibling `close` in between. The last word is the record's
            // `stillOpen` probe — a view of this object's own state, consulted
            // by the runtime INSIDE the posting step — which fails the read
            // with `canceledError` exactly as the close would have, had it
            // found the fiber parked. This check stays: it answers the common
            // case from the latch without a failure, and it is what keeps the
            // probe's `false` arm the rarity it should be.
            match self.state with
                case StreamState.Open =>
                    // The runtime lifts the bytes and, if the copy reported the
                    // stream ended, consults the result future — so an ending
                    // arrives ALONGSIDE whatever that same copy moved, never
                    // instead of it (3). A raised failure would have nowhere to
                    // carry the bytes, and the bytes are out of the pinned buffer
                    // already.
                    //
                    // So the failure channel here means one thing: this fiber was
                    // detached from the handle by a `close`. That is (4), it is
                    // not the transport, and it is not latched.
                    let copied = await Async<Array<Uint8>, E>.streamRead(self.source, capacity)
                    delivered = copied._1
                    match copied._2 with
                        case Some(error) =>
                            // The stream ended and the result future said why.
                            // Latch and stop: the tail hands over whatever this
                            // copy carried, and the NEXT read reports the reason.
                            self
                                .latchEnding(Some(error))
                            posting = false
                        case None =>
                            if copied._0 == CopyResult.Dropped then
                                // A clean end of stream, latched WHERE IT IS
                                // OBSERVED rather than after the loop: between
                                // the two the fiber can be descheduled, and an
                                // `Ended` written over a sibling close's
                                // `Released` makes `dropHandles` drop the handles
                                // twice, which traps.
                                let noReason: Option<E> = None
                                self.latchEnding(noReason)
                                posting = false
                            else
                                if delivered.length > 0 then
                                    // Bytes, and the stream is still open. Whether
                                    // the copy completed or was cancelled on the way,
                                    // they are the caller's — (3) again, and a cancel
                                    // that lost its race is exactly the copy that
                                    // carries some.
                                    posting = false
                                else
                                    if copied._0 == CopyResult.Cancelled then
                                        // Nothing came of it, and this is the one result
                                        // of the three that is about THIS OPERATION
                                        // rather than the transport: the copy was
                                        // cancelled out from under this fiber. Reported
                                        // with the same value the runtime raises in a
                                        // fiber it detaches. Retrying would send a
                                        // cancelled fiber back around a loop whose
                                        // patience runs out in a panic; returning empty
                                        // would report an interrupt as a clean end of
                                        // stream.
                                        await Async<Unit, E>.fail(self.source.canceledError)
                                    else
                                        // A copy that transferred nothing is not an end
                                        // of stream — only `Dropped` ends one — but every
                                        // caller reads an empty array as EOF, so handing
                                        // this back would stop `readAll` with a truncated
                                        // body. The stream is open and the bytes are still
                                        // coming: post again, boundedly. See
                                        // `emptyCopyLimit` for why there is a bound.
                                        emptyCopies =
                                            noteCopyProgress(
                                                emptyCopies,
                                                delivered.length,
                                                "input stream: a host copy transferred no bytes and did not end the stream"
                                            )
                case _ => posting = false
        // (3), and the whole of the answer: whatever the copy moved is the
        // caller's, whatever else that copy reported. With nothing moved there is
        // the latch — an end of stream, the failure that ended it, or the
        // `canceledError` of a stream somebody closed.
        if delivered.length > 0 then delivered
        else await Async.fromResult(self.latchedResult())

    /// Record that the stream has ended, and the transport's reason if it gave
    /// one. The result future is one-shot and the runtime has already consumed
    /// it, so every later read answers from here.
    ///
    /// It does NOT raise. The copy that observed the ending may also have carried
    /// bytes, and those are the caller's before the reason is (3).
    ///
    /// ONLY FROM `Open`, and that guard does two jobs. The first reader to see
    /// the ending records it; a straggler is reporting a consequence, not the
    /// cause. And a copy can settle while a sibling's `close` runs — the fiber is
    /// enqueued, not parked, between the two — so without the guard an `Ended`
    /// would overwrite that close's `Released`, and `dropHandles` would drop host
    /// handles it had already let go of. That traps.
    function latchEnding(self, reason: Option<E>): Unit =
        match self.state with
            case StreamState.Open =>
                self.state = StreamState.Ended
                self.finishError = reason
            case _ => ()

    /// The answer every read gives once the stream is no longer `Open`: the
    /// transport error that ended it, the `canceledError` of a stream somebody
    /// closed, or EOF. See `unusable` — this is that rule with an empty array
    /// standing in for "nothing wrong, and nothing left".
    function latchedResult(self): Result<Array<Uint8>, E> =
        match self.unusable() with
            case Some(e) => Error(e)
            case None => Ok(Array<Uint8>.empty())

    /// Drop the host handles this stream owns, silently and without cancelling
    /// anything.
    ///
    /// The *finalizer's* close, called by the `Resource` built alongside this
    /// object and by nobody else — which is why it is not `close` and not part of
    /// `AsyncInputStream`. It needs no cancel: a finalizer runs only after its
    /// scope has unwound every fiber it owns, and interrupting a fiber both
    /// cancels that fiber's copy and gives up its place in the handle's lock
    /// queue, so by the time this runs nothing is posted and nothing is queued.
    /// `close` below has no such guarantee and does the cancelling itself.
    ///
    /// Silent because a finalizer has nowhere to put an error, and idempotent
    /// because `close` drops these handles early and the Resource then releases
    /// over the top of it.
    public function dropHandles(self): Unit =
        match self.state with
            case StreamState.Released => ()
            case _ =>
                self.state = StreamState.Released
                // The handles are about to go, so the record's probe must say
                // so before they do — the finalizer path enters here straight
                // from `Open`, without passing `close`.
                self
                    .openFlag
                    .open = false
                // The runtime reads and drops the paired result future when a
                // copy reports the stream ended. Closing a stream that never
                // ended leaves that to `dropEnd`, which drops both halves.
                self
                    .source
                    .dropEnd()

    /// Detach every fiber from this stream's readable end, then drop it.
    ///
    /// Nothing is reported, and there is nothing it could report: every transport
    /// failure on this side arrives on the read that met it, unlike the write
    /// side's late error. The error channel is here for symmetry with
    /// `AsyncOutputStream.close` and for subclasses that do have something to say.
    ///
    /// THREE STEPS, in this order, for every state that still owns the handle —
    /// `Open` and `Ended` alike.
    ///
    /// LEAVE the current state for `Closing` first, synchronously, before
    /// anything can yield. It stops a fiber arriving fresh at `read` from posting
    /// a copy the drop would have to trap on, and it is the signal a concurrent
    /// close reads.
    ///
    /// CANCEL second, and only then, because the detached fibers are merely
    /// enqueued and none of them touches the handle on its way out. It is needed
    /// on an `Ended` stream too: a reader that reached the runtime's per-handle
    /// lock queue while an earlier read was posted is still waiting there, and
    /// would be granted the lock — and post — the moment the holder lets go, on
    /// whatever index the drop has by then handed back to the host.
    ///
    /// DROP last.
    ///
    /// `Released` is the one state where none of this may happen: the handle is
    /// gone and its index reused, so a cancel aimed at it would detach whatever
    /// fibers are on some other stream. That is what makes a second close safe.
    ///
    /// `Closing` does NOTHING: another close is between its cancel and its drop,
    /// and dropping the handles from here would pull them out from under it. The
    /// window is small — a close yields only if it exhausts its step budget
    /// mid-way — and real. The first close finishes the job.
    public override async function close(self): Async<Unit, E> =
        match self.state with
            case StreamState.Closing => ()
            case StreamState.Released => ()
            case _ => await self.detachAndDrop().uninterruptible()

    /// The LATCH → CANCEL → DROP sequence, run as ONE uninterruptible unit.
    ///
    /// Once `state` is latched to `Closing` and the probe flipped, the cancel and
    /// the drop MUST both run: an interrupt landing between the latch and the
    /// `CancelStream` node — reachable when the close straddles the fairness
    /// budget's boundary — would otherwise discard the rest of this method, and
    /// with it the `detachFibersFrom` that drains the handle's lock queue and the
    /// `dropHandles` that releases the handle. A queued waiter is then left on a
    /// handle nothing will ever drain, and the handle itself leaks — permanently
    /// on a stream nobody's `Resource` finalizer backstops. Masking makes the
    /// three steps atomic against interruption; the interrupt is not lost, only
    /// deferred to the mask's pop, exactly as a bracket release defers one. The
    /// region has no wait of its own, so masking it delays nothing real.
    async function detachAndDrop(self): Async<Unit, E> =
        self.state = StreamState.Closing
        // Flip the probe in the same synchronous breath as the state: from here
        // to the drop, a copy-posting step anywhere else must see `stillOpen`
        // false, or it posts on a handle this close is about to take away.
        self
            .openFlag
            .open = false
        await Async<Array<Uint8>, E>.cancelStreamRead(self.source)
        self.dropHandles()

module WasiInputStream<E> =
    /// A p3 read stream as a `Resource`: acquiring opens it, releasing drops the
    /// handles it owns.
    ///
    /// The only way to get one, deliberately. The stream object owns host handles
    /// that nothing else knows how to release, so a constructor anyone could call
    /// would be a public way to leak them — and under p3 a leaked handle is worse
    /// than a leak, because the host reuses the index and a later operation lands
    /// on somebody else's stream.
    ///
    /// Pairing acquisition with release here is also what lets the value handed
    /// out be the abstract type: `Resource.map` widens it while keeping the
    /// finalizer intact, so a caller gets an `AsyncInputStream` that closes itself
    /// and never has to know what is underneath.
    public function resource<E2>(open: () => Async<ComponentStreamRead<Array<Uint8>, E>, E2>):
        Resource<AsyncInputStream<E>, E2>
        where E: IoError =
        // An `async` acquire rather than a `map`, so that `Mutex.make` can be the
        // effect it is.
        let acquire: () => Async<WasiInputStream<E>, E2> =
            async () =>
                let raw = await open()
                // The probe outlives this scope inside the record, so it closes
                // over a flag made first rather than over the stream object made
                // last — see `OpenFlag` for the construction-order knot this cuts.
                let openFlag = OpenFlag(true)
                let source = raw.withStillOpen(() => openFlag.open)
                let readLock = await Mutex.make()
                let noError: Option<E> = None
                WasiInputStream<E>(source, openFlag, StreamState.Open, noError, readLock)
        let held: Resource<WasiInputStream<E>, E2> =
            Resource.make(acquire, (s: WasiInputStream<E>) => Async.thunk(() => s.dropHandles()))
        held.map<AsyncInputStream<E>>((s: WasiInputStream<E>) => s)
